<section aria-labelledby="about-tool" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pt-16">
  <article class="prose-site">
    <h2 id="about-tool" style="margin-top:0">What a track split calculator does</h2>
    <p>A track split calculator turns a goal time into the time you should hit at the end of every lap. You enter a race distance and a goal. The tool divides the race into laps of 200 m, 300 m, 400 m or any length you set. It then lists the time for each lap and the running total. Coaches use it to set targets. Runners copy it onto a wrist band or a phone note.</p>
    <p>The same tool works the other way. Enter one lap time and it gives your finish time. It can also build an interval session, such as 8 x 400 m at your goal race pace.</p>

    <h2>How to use the calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick what you want: goal to splits, lap to finish, or intervals.</li>
      <li>Choose the race distance. The list has the common track and road races. Pick Custom to type your own in meters, kilometers or miles.</li>
      <li>Type the goal time, like 20:00 for a 5K. A plain number counts as minutes.</li>
      <li>Choose the lap length. A standard outdoor track is 400 m. Many indoor tracks are 200 m.</li>
      <li>Pick even, negative or positive pacing. For a negative split, set how many seconds faster the second half should be.</li>
      <li>Read the table, then print the pace band or copy the splits.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The math in one line</h2>
    <p>Time per lap = goal time &divide; race distance &times; lap length. For a 5K in 20:00 on a 400 m track: 1200 s &divide; 5000 m &times; 400 m = 96 s, or 1:36.0 per lap. The race is 12.5 laps, so the last split covers 200 m and takes 48.0 s.</p>
    <p>One mile is exactly 1609.344 m, by the 1959 international yard and pound agreement. On a 400 m track a mile is 4 laps plus 9.34 m. A mile in 4:00 is 59.7 s per 400 m.</p>

    <h2>Even, negative and positive splits</h2>
    <p>An even split holds the same pace on every lap. A negative split means the second half of the race is faster than the first. A positive split means it is slower. An even or slightly negative pace is a common plan for races from the mile up, because a start that is too fast often costs more time late in the race than it saves.</p>
    <p>This calculator changes pace in a straight line across the race, so there are no sudden jumps between laps. You set the gap between the two halves. It keeps your total time exact. The tool rounds each running total to the nearest 0.1 s (or 1 s), then takes the lap times as differences, so the laps always add up to your goal.</p>
    <p>Example: 5K in 20:00 with the second half 6 s faster. Lap 1 is 1:36.9, lap 6 is 1:36.1 and the lap at 4,800 m is 1:35.2. The halves are 10:03.0 and 9:57.0.</p>

